## ✅ **Selection Sort (Simple & Non-Adaptive)**

### 🔹 Code (C)
```c
#include <stdio.h>

void selectionSort(int a[], int n) {
    int i, j, min, temp;
    for(i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        min = i;
        for(j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
            if(a[j] < a[min])
                min = j;
        }
        temp = a[i];
        a[i] = a[min];
        a[min] = temp;
    }
}

int main() {
    int a[] = {64, 25, 12, 22, 11};
    int n = sizeof(a)/sizeof(a[0]);

    selectionSort(a, n);

    printf("Sorted: ");
    for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
}
```

### 📌 Time Complexity
| Case | Complexity |
|------|------------|
| Best | O(n²) |
| Average | O(n²) |
| Worst | O(n²) |

→ Because always **n*(n-1)/2 comparisons**, no matter if sorted.

